# KNOWN GAP (t0556) — the SELF-HOST silently SWALLOWS a keyword that has no
# parse rule, together with its block AND every statement after it.
#
# THE MECHANISM, which is a CLASS and not one keyword. `self_host_typechecker/
# parser.gg` gates the named-scope path on `if self.check_tok(TOK_IDENT)`. A
# word that lexes as a KEYWORD is never TOK_IDENT, so if no parse rule consumes
# it, control falls past the named-scope path into `parse_expr()`, which eats
# the word, its colon, its indented body, and the rest of the enclosing block.
# Core #10 lower-or-reject: the SH must `lower_fail` LOUDLY on a construct it
# does not implement, never emit silently-truncated code.
#
# `where` is the surviving costume: it is a keyword on BOTH lanes
# (`src/lexer/token.rs`; `self_host_typechecker/lexer.gg`) and `Keyword::Where`
# has ZERO consumers outside the lexer, so no parse rule claims it.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE CLASS, NOT THE INSTANCE D50 CLOSED. The `unsafe:` costume of the
# identical mechanism was retired by REMOVING the keyword (D50, 2026-08-28) —
# which fixed that instance and left the mechanism untouched. Any keyword added
# to the lexer without a parse rule reopens it.
#
# ⚠ THE PIN MUST RUN ON THE SELF-HOST DRIVER (`sh_known_gap_expect`), NOT on
# `check_gg_fails`: the RUST lane already rejects `where:` with a parse error,
# so a `gg check` pin is green on arrival and asserts NOTHING about this gap.
#
# MEASURED on the self-host lowerer driver (`sh_known_gap_expect`) at the D50
# removal commit: stdout `"a\nb"`, exit 0. So the block BODY does run, and it
# is the statements AFTER the construct — `print("c")` and `print("d")` — that
# are silently swallowed. Rust gg rejects the same program with 10 parse
# errors, so the two lanes disagree about whether this program exists at all.
#
# INTENDED (what a correct self-host does): reject loudly, the way Rust gg
# does. The expected output below is the CORRECT/intended output for the
# construct's shape — the whole point is that the SH silently produces less.
void main():
    print("a")
    where:
        print("b")
    print("c")
    print("d")
